        "3:12",
        "3:13",
        "3:14"
      ]
    },
    {
      "ordinal": 5,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "2.2",
      "title": "Description",
      "body": "Falchions existed in many forms throughout the Middle Ages.\nThey have been classified by James Elmslie in 2015, and we will stick to his typology for buhurt.\n\nType of Falchions:\n\n● Type 1 A straight blade with a wider part on the striking edge.\n\n● Type 2 “Reverse-edged” forward curve, the point must be rounded or removed.\n\n● Type 3 Variations in having scalloped or cutaway tips on the blades.\n\n● Type 4 Curved saber-like blades often associated with, but not exclusive to, Eastern Europe.\n\n● Type 5 Symmetrical single-edged profiles with little to no curvature.\n\n● 2-handed Not classified by James Elmslie , but depicted in manuscripts.\n\nFalchions that are not based on the James Elmslie typology can be accepted if documented by historical sources.",
      "page_from": 3,
      "page_to": 3,
      "stable_key": "falchions:2.2",
      "parent_key": "falchions:2",
