    {
      "ordinal": 26,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "10.1",
      "title": "Pommels",
      "body": "Pommels can be of many forms and designs, mainly of rounded or square shape.\nPommels shapes are sorted under the Oakeshott Typology.\nPommels can be decorated.\nAny metal part of the pommel must conform to the “Armors and Weapons Aesthetics and Decorations” document.\n\nExample of historical shapes from OakeshottTypology:\n\nTypes A to E are all Denied as they are from centuries that predate the 14th century.\n\nTypes G1 and G2 originated from the 16th century.\nThey are:\n● Approved for 16th century armors and weapons.\n● Denied for 14th century, Transitional and 15th century armors and weapons.\n\nFor safety reason, it is Denied to reproduce a hook shape for the pommel, despite it is documented by historical sources, such as:",
