    {
      "ordinal": 27,
      "depth": 1,
      "kind": "rule",
      "number": "10.2",
      "title": "Guards",
      "body": "Guards can be of many forms and designs, mainly of straight design for 14th and 15th century designs.\nIt is recommended to avoid exotic shapes of guards and stick to the classic shapes, widely supported by historical sources.\nGuards can be decorated.\nAny metal part of the pommel must conform to the “Armors and Weapons Aesthetics and Decorations” document.\n\nExample of historical shapes from Oakeshott Typology:\n\n© Ewatt Oakeshott - The Sword in the Age of Chivalry\n\nNote: Oakeshott defined up to 12 different styles of guards, with variations.\nWe selected only the first 6 styles to keep the document simple, but other styles of guards from the Oakeshott typology are acceptable to use.",
